---
import UseCase from "../../layouts/UseCase.astro"

const faqs = [
	{
		question: "Does the simulation engine implement the full BPMN spec?",
		answer:
			"@bpmnkit/engine covers the parts of BPMN that matter for realistic process testing: service and user tasks, exclusive/parallel/inclusive gateways, timers, message correlation, and DMN business-rule evaluation.",
	},
	{
		question: "Can I run this in a browser, not just Node.js?",
		answer:
			'Yes — @bpmnkit/engine has zero runtime dependencies and runs the same way in the browser or Node.js, so it can back an in-browser "test this process" feature as well as a CI test suite.',
	},
]
---

<UseCase
  title="Simulate and test a BPMN process"
  description="Run a BPMN process end-to-end without a Camunda deployment — @bpmnkit/engine is a zero-dependency simulation engine for fast, deterministic process tests."
  slug="process-simulation"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>
    Testing a process by deploying it to Camunda, starting an instance, and watching Operate
    is slow feedback for something that should behave like a unit test. <code>@bpmnkit/engine</code>
    runs the same BPMN 2.0 XML you'd deploy to Camunda 8 entirely in-process, so a process test
    runs in milliseconds.
  </p>

  <h2>Writing a process test</h2>
  <pre>{`import { Engine } from "@bpmnkit/engine";

const engine = new Engine();
await engine.deploy({ bpmn: xml });

engine.registerJobWorker("payment-charge", async (job) => {
  await job.complete({ chargeId: "test-123" });
});

const instance = engine.start("payment-flow", {
  variables: { orderId: "ord-1", amount: 42 },
});

// assert the instance reached the expected end event, took the expected
// gateway branch, and completed with the expected variables`}</pre>
  <p>
    Because job workers are just functions you register, a test can simulate a failing
    payment, a timeout, or any other edge case without touching a real external system —
    useful for exercising <a href="https://docs.bpmnkit.com/guides/patterns/">boundary-event
    error paths</a> that are hard to trigger reliably against a live integration.
  </p>

  <h2>Where this fits in a pipeline</h2>
  <p>
    Generate a process with <code>@bpmnkit/core</code>, exercise it with
    <code>@bpmnkit/engine</code>, and only deploy to Camunda 8 with <code>@bpmnkit/api</code>
    once the simulation passes — the same shape as the
    <a href="/use-cases/camunda-8-automation">Camunda 8 automation</a> pipeline, with a fast
    correctness gate in the middle.
  </p>

  <div class="uc-links">
    <a href="https://docs.bpmnkit.com/packages/engine/">@bpmnkit/engine reference</a>
    <a href="https://docs.bpmnkit.com/guides/simulation/">Simulation guide</a>
    <a href="/blog/simulate-bpmn-process-without-camunda">Simulate a BPMN process without Camunda</a>
  </div>
</UseCase>
